From 959f60dc573771db593268aa87bb385212ea73d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 21:35:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 87/87] vulkan/wsi/Horizon: the copy fallback's dequeue loop
 can end
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 3 (zero-copy scanout)
Why: 0071 gave the copy fallback its own retry loop around
 nwindowDequeueBuffer, `for (;;)` with nothing in it that can stop.
 "Would block" is an overloaded answer on this platform:
 wsi_horizon_dequeue_would_block accepts LibnxBinderError_NoInit, which
 is Android's NO_INIT/-ENODEV and is what BOTH a full queue and a
 disconnected one return, and LibnxBinderError_InvalidOperation.
 Retrying is right for the full queue and never terminates for the
 disconnected one — so a window another swapchain has taken, or a lost
 device, leaves the application inside vkQueuePresentKHR for good.
 `said_stuck` only stops the warning repeating; the
 VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR the caller would act on is below the loop and
 is never reached.
Evidence: wsi_horizon.c as 0071 left it, the whole of the loop's exit
 logic:
   if (!wsi_horizon_dequeue_would_block(rc)) { ...; return NULL; }
   if (!said_stuck && ...) { mesa_logw(...); said_stuck = true; }
   svcSleepThread(WSI_HORIZON_REFRESH_NS / 8);
 The zero-copy acquire loop, 500 lines above, asks two more questions
 every round and says why at length: `wsi_horizon_presentable` for a
 window taken from under it — "What separates them is ownership, and
 ownership is knowable" — and `vk_device_is_lost` for a device that
 cannot free a buffer either, added after run 14 hung on exactly this.
 The same two are asked here.
 Class X: cross-built 2026-09-08 with this tree's flags,
 aarch64-none-elf-gcc (devkitA64) 15.2.0, no warning. No console has
 hung on this path; what a console HAS shown is the abort this loop
 replaced, three times, quoted in the function's header.
Upstream: no — the backend is Horizon's.

A live, owned window whose buffers are all queued is still waited on,
because that is a real wait and vkQueuePresentKHR has no timeout in its
signature. What ends now is the wait for something that is never
coming, and it ends as NULL, which the caller already turns into
VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR.
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 41 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 41 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index 83ea967..3f1fefd 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -2683,6 +2683,47 @@ wsi_horizon_fb_begin(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain, u32 *out_stride)
          return NULL;
       }
 
+      /* AND THE SAME TWO WAYS OUT, WHICH THIS LOOP DID NOT HAVE.
+       *
+       * "Would block" is an overloaded answer here: LibnxBinderError_
+       * NoInit is Android's NO_INIT/-ENODEV, which a FULL queue and a
+       * DISCONNECTED one both return, and InvalidOperation is the
+       * producer's allowance being spent. Retrying is right for the
+       * first and never terminates for the second, so a loop with
+       * neither a deadline nor a way to tell them apart hangs the
+       * application inside vkQueuePresentKHR — `said_stuck` only stops
+       * the log repeating, and the OUT_OF_DATE below is never reached.
+       *
+       * What separates them is ownership and health, which is
+       * knowable, and the zero-copy acquire loop already asks both
+       * questions every round for exactly this reason; the same two
+       * are asked here. A window another swapchain has taken answers
+       * NO_INIT forever and `presentable` says so. A lost device
+       * cannot free a buffer either, and vk_device_is_lost is an
+       * atomic read of a flag NVK already sets. A live, owned window
+       * whose buffers are all queued is a real wait and is still
+       * waited on, which is what a present with no timeout in its
+       * signature means.
+       *
+       * Both answers are NULL, which the caller turns into
+       * VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR — the result an application can act
+       * on, and the one this path already gives for its other
+       * failures. */
+      if (!wsi_horizon_presentable(chain)) {
+         mesa_logw("wsi_horizon: the copy fallback's window was taken "
+                   "while it waited for a buffer; last dequeue said "
+                   "0x%08x", rc);
+         return NULL;
+      }
+
+      VK_FROM_HANDLE(vk_device, device, chain->base.device);
+      if (vk_device_is_lost(device)) {
+         mesa_logw("wsi_horizon: the device was lost while the copy "
+                   "fallback waited for a buffer; last dequeue said "
+                   "0x%08x", rc);
+         return NULL;
+      }
+
       /* The same budget and the same idle as the zero-copy acquire, for
        * the same measured reason: the release event on this window is
        * permanently signalled, so a loop with no sleep in it is binder
-- 
